WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.300
There's nothing scarier than the truth.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.233
If you're a true believer 
in the paranormal

00:00:06.233 --> 00:00:09.233
then you likely know this couple, 
Ed and Lorraine Warren.

00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:11.000
And if you don't

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.900
you might have heard about movies
they inspired

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.166
like The Conjuring, Annabelle,
The Haunted, The Nun

00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:16.666
The Amityville Horror, 
The Haunting in Connecticut.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:20.433
Their story spaned a cinematic universe 
that rivals the success of...

00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:23.633
well, the main one you think of 
when I say the words

00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:25.000
cinematic universe.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.633
But this isn't the first time 
their stories are told.

00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:33.766
So how did this very specific, 
very scary Conjuring universe...

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.600
become so successful?

00:00:44.366 --> 00:00:46.166
Ed Warren was a demonologist.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.300
Lorraine Warren was a trance medium...

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.033
better known as a clairvoyant.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.366
Together, for decades, they were 
paranormal investigators...

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.166
looking to some of the most famous 
occurrences of the occult...

00:00:56.366 --> 00:00:57.600
all over the world.

00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:00.866
“This was just another haunted house,
and we didn't even realize...

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.500
that six people had been murdered 
in that house: The DeFoe family.”

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.333
They investigated hauntings 
of small children

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:10.133
demonic presences and 
even a werewolf...

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.466
which is all detailed on their 
incredible website

00:01:13.466 --> 00:01:16.800
that has case files for some of their 
most famous investigations.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.266
This one in particular was no joke.

00:01:19.266 --> 00:01:22.033
As Lorraine explains in this featurette
from Warner Brothers.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.600
“This is the worst thing.”

00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:26.033
“I'm not going to—I'm not going 
to stare at it, though.”

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.500
“That has done bad harm 
on a lot of people.”

00:01:29.766 --> 00:01:33.233
Now, why would they have a creepy doll 
on display like that?

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.633
Well, after the Warrens 
closed a case

00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:38.233
they would often take 
the haunted objects back home

00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:39.700
in order to keep them safe 
from the public.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.733
They kept them in their basement 
in Monroe, Connecticut...

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.533
which went on to become an occult museum
considered to be...

00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:47.833
“The most haunted area in the world.”

00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:48.833
“That's what I would say.”

00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:50.666
“I would say that it is.”

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.300
Without even mentioning Hollywood

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.833
I think anyone would say that their story has

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:57.433
all of the ingredients to make 
an awesome movie.

00:01:57.766 --> 00:02:00.300
But when Hollywood has tackled their cases...

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.266
most of the films have left the Warrens 
out of the narrative.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.300
Like there are roughly 
30 Amityville movies

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.300
about this haunted house where in 1976

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.366
the Warrens and their crew investigated...

00:02:11.566 --> 00:02:14.866
and took this ghost photo of a boy 
who had been murdered there.

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:20.466
But none of these films were 
specifically about the couple.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.533
The Warrens were portrayed
on the small screen

00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:24.000
in 1991’s The Haunted—

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.066
which can I just say, 
[chef’s kiss with Italian hands] excellent title.

00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:30.966
But they weren't put at the center 
of any story until 2013’s...

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:32.233
The Conjuring.

00:02:32.266 --> 00:02:35.366
The movie that changed everything 
for the horror genre.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.333
And that's not hyperbole.

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:41.466
The Conjuring is the highest grossing 
horror franchise of all time.

00:02:41.766 --> 00:02:45.066
But before we dive into the 
boring financials of the universe...

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.100
let's first talk about why 
The Conjuring worked.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.600
The trailer for the film lays on 
the concept of candor really thick.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:56.766
“It's not a haunting.”

00:02:56.766 --> 00:02:57.966
“It's not a possession.”

00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.366
“But the truth.”

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.166
It was marketing gold, 
of course.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.300
But the truth was also incredibly important 
to the creative team.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.600
Lorraine Warren was a consultant on the film

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.333
and she became close friends 
with her onscreen counterpart.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.866
The subjects of the film, 
the Perron family

00:03:13.866 --> 00:03:16.333
who were haunted and eventually possessed 

00:03:16.333 --> 00:03:18.200
by a witch named Bathsheba...

00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:20.766
are still very much alive 
and have given

00:03:20.766 --> 00:03:22.800
chilling interviews with Warner Brothers.

00:03:23.066 --> 00:03:24.733
Like in this other featurette.

00:03:24.866 --> 00:03:28.700
“I doubt very much that 
I will be willing to see the film...

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.300
for the same reason I'm unwilling 
to ever go back to the house.”

00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:35.466
Jesus Christ.

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:38.833
Of course, creative liberties 
are always taken

00:03:38.833 --> 00:03:41.366
but the team had a lot of reverence 
for the truth.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.700
Reflecting on the film recently 
with Entertainment Weekly.

00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:51.633
Director James Wan said that 
one of the things he wanted to do

00:03:51.633 --> 00:03:53.766
is to treat it like it's straight forward drama

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.433
and not even a horror film.

00:03:55.466 --> 00:03:58.766
And that's pretty clear because the story is heartfelt...

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.366
the acting is sincere and grounded,
and it was masterfully shot.

00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:04.833
This is true of pretty much every movie

00:04:04.833 --> 00:04:06.033
in The Conjuring universe.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.866
They all try to keep an emotional story at the center...

00:04:09.066 --> 00:04:11.666
whether it's romantic, familial, 
or friendship...

00:04:11.966 --> 00:04:14.666
and they all have extremely creative scares.

00:04:17.366 --> 00:04:19.366
And if you have beef 
with that statement

00:04:19.366 --> 00:04:21.466
then you can take it up 
with my electric bill.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.600
Because after rewatching the movies
to make this video...

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.433
I have not been able to sleep with light off for days

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:30.033
and I really wish I was joking.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.100
But I am so scared.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.433
The first 10 minutes of The Conjuring 
are primarily focused on

00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:41.500
building out who the Warrens are 
through the Annabelle case.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.666
You know the one about 
the extremely haunted doll

00:04:44.666 --> 00:04:46.433
that Lorraine was talking about earlier?

00:04:46.433 --> 00:04:48.766
“That harmed a lot of people.”

00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:51.833
It looks a little different in the movie...

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.066
but it's a great way to establish that

00:04:54.066 --> 00:04:57.066
Ed and Lorraine are accomplished 
paranormal investigators...

00:04:57.266 --> 00:04:58.300
while giving the audience

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:00.100
a quick hit of scares before
moving on

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.700
to the actual subject of the movie:

00:05:01.966 --> 00:05:03.166
The Perron family.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.700
But also plants seeds to show

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.000
that there are more stories 
to tell in this world.

00:05:07.066 --> 00:05:09.566
Like in The Conjuring, we get
glimpses of Annabell.

00:05:09.766 --> 00:05:10.966
In The Conjuring 2, we get

00:05:10.966 --> 00:05:13.400
a few moments with the zoetrope
of the Crooked Man.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:14.666
In Annabelle Creations

00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:17.100
we get a photo that briefly features the nun.

00:05:17.366 --> 00:05:19.733
It's like the films are constantly referencing

00:05:19.733 --> 00:05:21.600
their deep archive of case files.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.000
But of course, no device is more beautiful

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.200
for building out a universe 
than the occult museum.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.600
It's like the Infinity Stones times a million.

00:05:31.866 --> 00:05:33.600
And the film's creators knew this.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:37.233
Director James Wan even said 
that it occurred to them early on.

00:05:37.433 --> 00:05:39.466
“There's already a superhero universe.”

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.700
“Why couldn't they do that in the horror world?”

00:05:45.633 --> 00:05:47.366
When you think of your favorite horror films

00:05:47.366 --> 00:05:50.066
they're often continuously running franchises.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.466
People liked Halloween.

00:05:51.466 --> 00:05:53.600
So let's give them more Halloween.

00:05:53.966 --> 00:05:55.766
Same with Nightmare on Elm Street.

00:05:55.866 --> 00:05:57.566
But not The Conjuring.

00:05:57.933 --> 00:06:00.600
One year after its release, 
Annabelle came out...

00:06:00.766 --> 00:06:03.866
giving a feature length treatment 
to the minor story

00:06:03.866 --> 00:06:05.400
at the beginning of The Conjuring.

00:06:05.966 --> 00:06:08.733
That would be like if Scream 
was followed up by a spin-off

00:06:08.733 --> 00:06:11.100
of the history of Drew Barrymore's character.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.433
Who—spoiler alert for a 
27-year-old-movie—

00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:16.733
dies in the first 15 minutes of the film.

00:06:16.866 --> 00:06:18.866
But of course, the filmmakers didn't do that.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.766
They just did Scream 2, 
which followed characters

00:06:21.766 --> 00:06:24.300
from Scream 1 on a 
new slashing adventure.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.833
Like the MCU, The Conjuring 
expands the universe around its characters.

00:06:29.166 --> 00:06:31.200
The Warrens, Annabelle, 
and the Nun...

00:06:31.466 --> 00:06:33.466
all have their own standalone francises

00:06:33.466 --> 00:06:35.766
which have connective tissue across movies

00:06:35.766 --> 00:06:38.066
but then they'll also crossover.

00:06:38.233 --> 00:06:41.466
Like how the Warrens show up 
in the final Annabelle movie.

00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:43.800
The strength of doing a shared universe

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.100
is that you get to bounce around

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.866
with completely different vibes.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.366
But there's one more thing that

00:06:52.366 --> 00:06:54.900
makes the Conjuring universe stand out 
as a massive success.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.633
They make a huge bang for their buck

00:06:57.633 --> 00:06:59.833
thanks to their reasonably small budgets.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.633
This is because they're often 
haunted house films.

00:07:02.766 --> 00:07:04.766
And when you're mainly using one location...

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:08.866
you have fewer variables, more control,
and can shoot way faster.

00:07:09.466 --> 00:07:12.266
And they're not just successful 
by horror metrics.

00:07:12.466 --> 00:07:15.866
Compare the return on investment 
of the budget versus the box office

00:07:15.866 --> 00:07:17.400
to other shared universes...

00:07:17.633 --> 00:07:21.300
and the results are impressive, 
to say the least.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.833
And Warren passed away in 2006.

00:07:25.233 --> 00:07:27.600
Lorraine Warren passed away in 2019.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.866
In their 61 years of marriage

00:07:29.866 --> 00:07:33.300
the couple investigated over 10,000 cases.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.033
With no sign of The Conjuring universe's 
popularity slowing down...

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.100
there are still so many stories left to tell.

00:07:40.966 --> 00:07:42.700
Hey, it's me!

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.233
The ghost that's come out to haunt you 
forever and ever.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.766
Before I do that, I was told to 
read this card.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.000
So let's see what it says here, huh?

00:07:51.766 --> 00:07:52.966
First off, it says:

00:07:53.066 --> 00:07:54.933
Thank you so much for watching this video.

00:07:55.466 --> 00:07:56.866
I have no idea what that means, but...

00:07:57.566 --> 00:07:59.766
anyways, weird, 
it also says...

00:08:00.166 --> 00:08:03.066
if you were to go to Vox.com/givenow

00:08:03.066 --> 00:08:04.566
and become a contributor...

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.600
I might not be forced to haunt you 
for all eternity.

00:08:08.166 --> 00:08:11.633
You'd also receive fun extras like 
a newsletter from Vox journalists.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.400
Apparently this is the best way 
to support our journalism

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.233
and make sure that Vox stays free.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.400
Also, it'll keep me from hovering 
around your room in the dead of night.

00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:24.400
So if you want to see more videos like this
and get some sleep...

00:08:24.733 --> 00:08:26.566
go to vox.com/givenow.

00:08:26.966 --> 00:08:28.166
See you in the next one.

00:08:28.333 --> 00:08:29.066
Or tonight.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:30.000
In your bedroom.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.266
If you don't contribute

00:08:31.266 --> 00:08:33.000
I'll be there and I'm going 
to haunt you.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:34.866
Beware.

00:08:35.166 --> 00:08:35.933
Alright, bye!

